## Canonical body structure

```markdown
## What happened

<One paragraph — the episode at a glance.>

## Context / trigger

<What was happening before that made this episode occur.
Principal-directive quote if applicable.>

## Flow

<Chronological (numbered) account of phases / decisions / pivots.
Not code-level; arc-level.  Each phase links out to any memorial
it produced or touched.>

## Artifacts produced

<Explicit list of created / updated memorials with one-line
summaries.  Duplicates `touches:` frontmatter for narrative
readability.>

## Surprises / blockers / pivots

<Non-happy-path material — discipline catches, sandbox blocks,
mid-phase direction changes.  This is the stuff commit messages
don't capture.>

## Lessons / rules surfaced

<If the episode produced any new feedback, preferences, protocols,
observations — name them here with links.>

## Open ends

<What remains.  Explicitly.  A future session re-reading this log
should know what's still unfinished.>

## See also

<Cross-links; primary memorials + adjacent logs.>
```

## Interlinking discipline

**Logs are value-dense only when interlinked.**  Every memorial the log cites should link back:

- Created memorials should cite the log as their landing context: `related: [logs/<date>_<slug>.md]`
- Updated memorials may cite the log in See-also if the update is substantive
- Subsequent logs building on earlier logs should cite ancestor logs

This reciprocity turns the log subtree into a **navigation surface** over the topical memory corpus — answering "what happened, in what order, producing what?"

## Lifecycle

Logs are **semi-archival**:

- Created at end-of-episode
- Rarely re-reviewed (no `last-reviewed` churn)
- Superseded only if the log itself turned out to misremember the episode
- Referenced from plan memorials (§8 retrospective), from future logs, from arc-reconstruction work
- **Never deleted** — the forensic value grows with time

## Dual role: handoff artifact

Logs serve a second role beyond chronological diary: **they are handoff artifacts** read by session-orientation tooling to bootstrap the next session.  This dual role brings extra discipline:

- **Versioned shape** — logs carry a `handoff-schema-version` frontmatter field naming which structural shape they were written under
- **Reified coordinates** — explicit `closed-at:` (ISO-8601 with offset), `host:`, and `environment:` in frontmatter, plus an HHMM time component in the filename (`<YYYY-MM-DD>T<HHMM>_<slug>.md`)

The picker targets the current version; legacy support is best-effort for a transition window then dropped.

## Pitfalls

- **Logs as diary** — writing a log after every session erodes signal; logs are event-triggered
- **Log without interlinks** — a log that doesn't cite its artifacts is a reminiscence, not a memorial.  Aim for heavy `related:` + body-level links
- **Log as replacement for patterns / observations** — if you found a pattern, write the pattern.  The log cites the pattern; it doesn't replace the pattern
- **Stale touches** — if `touches:` names memorials that have been deleted/renamed, refresh on re-read

## Composition with adjacent classes

- **`memoir`** — methodological sibling; memoir is process-record, log is session-record
- **`plan`** — a plan's `## Retrospective` section may cite logs documenting the arc's execution
- **`decision`** — the log that produced a decision records when + under what context it landed
- **`observation`** — observations surfaced during a logged episode should cite the log in their `## How it was noticed` section

